\chapter{Views}\label{ch:views}

The views are deliberately thin. Every number they show comes from the [[Session]] of
Chapter~\ref{ch:model}: a view asks it for the variants, the shared line, a profile or a spectrum,
and draws the answer. What the views do own is \emph{interaction}: which pane shows which variant,
how a drag on an image turns into a new line, and how the controls in each zone header write back
into the session. Because the session is an observable object, writing the line from one pane is
enough to redraw every image, profile and spectrum in the window.

The chapter follows the window from the outside in: the window and its three zones, the panes and
their tabs, the image canvas where the line is drawn, the two charts, and then the auxiliary
windows (the parameter sheet, the inspector and the test-pattern windows).

\section{The document window}

A document window is three zones stacked vertically: images, line profile, spectrum. The window
owns one [[Session]], created from the document when the window opens.

<<LineScope/Views/MainView.swift>>=
import LineScopeCore
import SwiftUI

struct MainView: View {
    <<MainView: the session is created once per window>>

    var body: some View {
        <<MainView: three zones stacked vertically>>
        <<MainView: publishing the session to menus and inspector>>
        <<MainView: sheets, alerts and the progress indicator>>
    }
}

<<MainView.swift: the window observer>>
@ SwiftUI may call a view's initializer many times, but a [[@State]] property is initialized only
the first time; later values passed to [[State(initialValue:)]] are ignored. That is exactly the
lifetime we want: one session per window, surviving every redraw.

<<MainView: the session is created once per window>>=
@State private var session: Session

init(document: ImageDocument, fileName: String) {
    _session = State(initialValue: Session(document: document, fileName: fileName))
}
@ The zones sit in a [[VSplitView]], so the user can drag the dividers between them. The ideal
heights give the images most of the room at first; the minimum sizes keep every zone's header and
at least a sliver of its content visible.

<<MainView: three zones stacked vertically>>=
VSplitView {
    ZoneView(zone: .images, session: session)
        .frame(minHeight: 180, idealHeight: 420)
    ZoneView(zone: .profile, session: session)
        .frame(minHeight: 140, idealHeight: 220)
    ZoneView(zone: .spectrum, session: session)
        .frame(minHeight: 140, idealHeight: 220)
}
.frame(minWidth: 720, minHeight: 560)
@ Two other parts of the app need to find this window's session. The menus
(Chapter~\ref{ch:commands}) read it as a \emph{focused scene value}, which SwiftUI provides for the
key window. The inspector cannot do that. It is a window of its own, and while the user works in it
\emph{it} is the key window, so the focused value would vanish just when it is needed. Instead, the
document window reports itself to the shared [[ActiveSession]] whenever it becomes key, and
withdraws when it closes (but only if it is still the active one, since another window may have
become key in between).

<<MainView: publishing the session to menus and inspector>>=
.focusedSceneValue(\.session, session)
.background(WindowKeyObserver(
    onBecomeKey: { ActiveSession.shared.session = session },
    onClose: {
        if ActiveSession.shared.session === session { ActiveSession.shared.session = nil }
    }
))
@ The session also drives three transient pieces of UI. Setting its [[pendingRequest]] presents the
parameter sheet (Section~\ref{sec:parameter-sheet}). Setting its [[errorMessage]] shows an alert,
and dismissing the alert clears the message again through the hand-made binding. While background
operations run, a small spinner appears in the toolbar.

<<MainView: sheets, alerts and the progress indicator>>=
.sheet(item: $session.pendingRequest) { request in
    ParameterSheet(request: request, session: session)
}
.alert(
    "Operation failed",
    isPresented: Binding(get: { session.errorMessage != nil }, set: { if !$0 { session.errorMessage = nil } }),
    presenting: session.errorMessage
) { _ in
    Button("OK") {}
} message: { Text($0) }
.toolbar {
    ToolbarItem(placement: .status) {
        if session.runningOperations > 0 {
            ProgressView().controlSize(.small)
        }
    }
}
@ SwiftUI has no notification for ``this window became key''. So we place an invisible AppKit view
in the background of the window's content. An [[NSView]] knows its [[NSWindow]], and through it can
observe the window's notifications. The representable just forwards the two callbacks.

<<MainView.swift: the window observer>>=
/// Reports when the hosting window becomes key or closes.
struct WindowKeyObserver: NSViewRepresentable {
    var onBecomeKey: () -> Void
    var onClose: () -> Void

    func makeNSView(context: Context) -> ObserverView { ObserverView() }

    func updateNSView(_ view: ObserverView, context: Context) {
        view.onBecomeKey = onBecomeKey
        view.onClose = onClose
    }

    <<WindowKeyObserver: the observing NSView>>
}
@ The view learns its window in [[viewDidMoveToWindow]], which also runs if the view ever moves to
another window, so old observers are removed first. Notifications are delivered on the main queue.
The closures are nonisolated as far as the compiler knows, so [[MainActor.assumeIsolated]] states
what is true at run time. A window that is \emph{already} key when the view arrives won't send
[[didBecomeKeyNotification]] again, so that case is reported directly, one run-loop turn later, after
SwiftUI has finished installing the view.

<<WindowKeyObserver: the observing NSView>>=
final class ObserverView: NSView {
    var onBecomeKey: () -> Void = {}
    var onClose: () -> Void = {}
    private var tokens: [NSObjectProtocol] = []

    override func viewDidMoveToWindow() {
        super.viewDidMoveToWindow()
        tokens.forEach(NotificationCenter.default.removeObserver)
        tokens = []
        guard let window else { return }
        let center = NotificationCenter.default
        tokens.append(center.addObserver(forName: NSWindow.didBecomeKeyNotification, object: window, queue: .main) { [weak self] _ in
            MainActor.assumeIsolated { self?.onBecomeKey() }
        })
        tokens.append(center.addObserver(forName: NSWindow.willCloseNotification, object: window, queue: .main) { [weak self] _ in
            MainActor.assumeIsolated { self?.onClose() }
        })
        if window.isKeyWindow {
            DispatchQueue.main.async { [weak self] in self?.onBecomeKey() }
        }
    }

    deinit {
        tokens.forEach(NotificationCenter.default.removeObserver)
    }
}
@

\section{Zones}

A zone is a header bar followed by one or more panes side by side. The panes of a zone are listed
in [[session.panes]], indexed by the zone's raw value, so splitting a zone is only a matter of
appending to that list. [[ZoneView]] is [[@Bindable]] because its header controls write straight
into the session.

<<LineScope/Views/ZoneView.swift>>=
import LineScopeCore
import SwiftUI

/// One horizontal zone: a header with its controls, and one or more side-by-side panes.
struct ZoneView: View {
    let zone: Zone
    @Bindable var session: Session

    <<ZoneView: header above side-by-side panes>>

    <<ZoneView: the header row>>

    <<ZoneView: controls specific to each zone>>

    <<ZoneView: split and unsplit buttons>>

    <<ZoneView: describing the line>>
}
@ The panes are laid out in an [[HSplitView]], so their widths can be adjusted by hand. Each pane
gets its index, which is how it finds its own entry in the session's pane list. The [[ForEach]] is
keyed by the pane's identity rather than its index, so removing the last pane doesn't rebuild
the others.

<<ZoneView: header above side-by-side panes>>=
var body: some View {
    VStack(spacing: 0) {
        header
            .padding(.horizontal, 10)
            .padding(.vertical, 5)
            .background(.bar)
        Divider()
        HSplitView {
            ForEach(Array(session.panes[zone.rawValue].enumerated()), id: \.element.id) { index, _ in
                PaneView(zone: zone, paneIndex: index, session: session)
                    .frame(minWidth: 200)
            }
        }
    }
}
@ Every header has the same shape: the zone's title on the left, then its own controls, then the
split buttons on the right.

<<ZoneView: the header row>>=
private var header: some View {
    HStack(spacing: 12) {
        Text(zone.title).font(.headline)
        Spacer(minLength: 8)
        controls
        Divider().frame(height: 16)
        splitButtons
    }
    .controlSize(.small)
}
@ The controls differ by zone.

<<ZoneView: controls specific to each zone>>=
@ViewBuilder
private var controls: some View {
    switch zone {
    case .images:
        <<ZoneView: image-zone controls>>
    case .profile:
        <<ZoneView: profile-zone controls>>
    case .spectrum:
        <<ZoneView: spectrum-zone controls>>
    }
}
@ The image zone shows where the line is and offers to put it back in its initial place: the full
width of the image, at mid-height.

<<ZoneView: image-zone controls>>=
Text(lineDescription)
    .font(.caption.monospacedDigit())
    .foregroundStyle(.secondary)
Button("Reset Line") { session.line = .initial }
@ The profile zone chooses the color model and the channel. They are settings of the
\emph{session}, not of a pane, so every profile and every spectrum in the window shows the same
channel. Comparisons between variants stay meaningful. The list of channels depends on the model
(RGBA has four, HSL three), so the second picker is rebuilt from [[colorModel.channels]].

<<ZoneView: profile-zone controls>>=
Picker("Color space", selection: $session.colorModel) {
    ForEach(ColorModel.allCases) { Text($0.displayName).tag($0) }
}
.pickerStyle(.segmented)
.fixedSize()
Picker("Channel", selection: $session.channelIndex) {
    ForEach(Array(session.colorModel.channels.enumerated()), id: \.offset) { i, name in
        Text(name).tag(i)
    }
}
.pickerStyle(.segmented)
.fixedSize()
@ The spectrum zone controls how the profile is transformed. There is the window function (a Hann
window~\cite{harris78} reduces the leakage caused by the finite line), the unit of the frequency
axis, whether to subtract the mean first (otherwise the DC term dwarfs everything else), and
whether amplitudes are shown in decibels.

<<ZoneView: spectrum-zone controls>>=
Picker("Window", selection: $session.window) {
    ForEach(WindowFunction.allCases) { Text($0.displayName).tag($0) }
}
.fixedSize()
Picker("Axis", selection: $session.frequencyAxis) {
    ForEach(FrequencyAxis.allCases) { Text($0.displayName).tag($0) }
}
.fixedSize()
Toggle("Remove mean", isOn: $session.removeMean)
Toggle("dB", isOn: $session.logMagnitude)
@ Splitting adds a pane and unsplitting removes the last one; a zone always keeps at least one.
The same actions are in the View menu.

<<ZoneView: split and unsplit buttons>>=
private var splitButtons: some View {
    HStack(spacing: 4) {
        Button {
            session.split(zone)
        } label: {
            Image(systemName: "rectangle.split.2x1")
        }
        .help("Split: add a pane to \(zone.title)")
        Button {
            session.unsplit(zone)
        } label: {
            Image(systemName: "rectangle")
        }
        .help("Unsplit: remove the last pane")
        .disabled(session.panes[zone.rawValue].count < 2)
    }
    .labelsHidden()
}
@ The line is stored in normalized coordinates, with $(0,0)$ at the top-left corner and $(1,1)$ at
the bottom-right corner of every image. So the same numbers describe it in all variants, whatever
their size. Three decimals are finer than a pixel for any image up to a thousand pixels wide.

<<ZoneView: describing the line>>=
private var lineDescription: String {
    let l = session.line
    func p(_ v: Double) -> String { v.formatted(.number.precision(.fractionLength(3))) }
    return "(\(p(l.start.x)), \(p(l.start.y))) → (\(p(l.end.x)), \(p(l.end.y)))"
}
@

\section{Panes and tabs}

Each pane shows one variant, chosen with a strip of tabs that lists all of them. The tab strips of
all panes list the same variants in the same order: the order in which they were made. What a pane
shows depends on its zone: the image, its profile, or its spectrum.

<<LineScope/Views/PaneView.swift>>=
import SwiftUI

/// A tab strip listing every variant, plus the zone's content for the selected one.
struct PaneView: View {
    let zone: Zone
    let paneIndex: Int
    let session: Session

    <<PaneView: which variant this pane shows>>

    <<PaneView: tab strip above the zone's content>>
}

<<PaneView.swift: the tab strip>>
@ A pane's selection is looked up rather than stored in the view, so it survives redraws and can be
changed by the session. When a variant is closed, the session moves every pane that showed it back
to the original. The fallbacks cover the brief moments during an update when a pane index or a
variant no longer exists: the original is always there.

<<PaneView: which variant this pane shows>>=
private var selectedID: UUID {
    let panes = session.panes[zone.rawValue]
    return paneIndex < panes.count ? panes[paneIndex].variantID : session.original.id
}
@ Selecting a tab also makes that variant the \emph{focused} one, the target of the next menu
operation.

<<PaneView: tab strip above the zone's content>>=
var body: some View {
    let variant = session.variant(selectedID) ?? session.original
    VStack(spacing: 0) {
        TabStrip(session: session, selectedID: variant.id) { id in
            session.select(id, zone: zone, pane: paneIndex)
        }
        Divider()
        Group {
            switch zone {
            case .images: ImageCanvas(variant: variant, session: session)
            case .profile: ProfileChart(variant: variant, session: session)
            case .spectrum: SpectrumChart(variant: variant, session: session)
            }
        }
        .frame(maxWidth: .infinity, maxHeight: .infinity)
    }
}
@ The tab strip scrolls horizontally when there are more variants than fit. Each tab is a plain
button whose label is drawn by hand. Its context menu offers the per-variant commands.

<<PaneView.swift: the tab strip>>=
private struct TabStrip: View {
    let session: Session
    let selectedID: UUID
    let onSelect: (UUID) -> Void

    var body: some View {
        ScrollView(.horizontal, showsIndicators: false) {
            HStack(spacing: 2) {
                ForEach(session.variants) { v in
                    let selected = v.id == selectedID
                    Button {
                        onSelect(v.id)
                    } label: {
                        <<TabStrip: a tab's label>>
                    }
                    .buttonStyle(.plain)
                    .help(v.name)
                    .contextMenu {
                        <<TabStrip: a tab's context menu>>
                    }
                }
            }
            .padding(.horizontal, 6)
            .padding(.vertical, 3)
        }
    }
}
@ A tab shows the variant's name (its chain of operations) and its pixel size, which is the quickest
way to tell a resampled copy from a filtered one. Two kinds of state are marked: a tinted
background for the variant this pane shows, and a small dot for the window's focused variant.
They differ as soon as a zone is split.

<<TabStrip: a tab's label>>=
HStack(spacing: 4) {
    if v.id == session.focusedVariantID {
        Circle().fill(Color.accentColor).frame(width: 5, height: 5)
    }
    Text(v.name).lineLimit(1)
    Text("\(v.buffer.width)×\(v.buffer.height)")
        .foregroundStyle(.secondary)
}
.font(.caption)
.padding(.horizontal, 8)
.padding(.vertical, 3)
.background(
    RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 5)
        .fill(selected ? Color.accentColor.opacity(0.22) : Color.clear)
)
.contentShape(Rectangle())
@ Export works on the focused variant, so the tab is selected first. The original can't be closed:
every other variant descends from it.

<<TabStrip: a tab's context menu>>=
Button("Export Image…") {
    onSelect(v.id)
    session.exportFocused()
}
Button("Close Tab") { session.closeVariant(v.id) }
    .disabled(v.isOriginal)
