\section{The image canvas}

The image zone must show each variant \emph{at its true scale}: one pixel of the image is one
point on the screen. A copy shrunk to half size then really looks half as big, and its blockiness
or blur is seen as it is, not hidden by the display's own interpolation. On a Retina display a
point is two device pixels, so by default each image pixel is a $2\times2$ block. The View menu's
``Actual Device Pixels'' option maps one image pixel to one device pixel instead.

On top of the image lies the selection line, which the user can drag.

<<LineScope/Views/ImageCanvas.swift>>=
import AppKit
import LineScopeCore
import SwiftUI

/// Shows a variant at its true scale (1 image pixel = 1 point, or 1 device pixel) with scrolling,
/// and lets the user drag the shared selection line.
struct ImageCanvas: View {
    let variant: ImageVariant
    @Bindable var session: Session
    @Environment(\.displayScale) private var displayScale
    @State private var drag: DragState?

    <<ImageCanvas: the state of a drag>>

    <<ImageCanvas: drawing the image at true scale>>

    <<ImageCanvas: the line gesture>>

    <<ImageCanvas: hit testing the line>>

    <<ImageCanvas: updating the line during a drag>>

    <<ImageCanvas: Shift snapping>>

    <<ImageCanvas: coordinate conversions>>
}

<<ImageCanvas.swift: the line overlay>>
@ A drag does one of four things, depending on where it starts. It moves the start point, moves
the end point, moves the whole segment, or draws a new segment from scratch. The line as it was
when the drag began is kept, because every update is computed from that initial line and the
gesture's total movement, never from the previous update. Small errors therefore cannot pile up
over a long drag.

<<ImageCanvas: the state of a drag>>=
private struct DragState {
    enum Mode { case start, end, whole, draw }
    var mode: Mode
    var initial: LineSegment
}
@ [[Image(decorative:scale:)]] takes the scale of the bitmap. A scale of 1 draws a pixel per point,
and the display's scale factor draws a pixel per device pixel. In both cases [[size]] is the
image's extent in points, and interpolation is turned off so enlarged pixels stay square.

The image and its overlay are given exactly that size, and the gesture is attached to that
fixed-size frame, \emph{inside} the scroll view. As a result the gesture's coordinates are
coordinates in the image. They don't depend on scrolling or on the empty margin around a small
image, and dividing by [[size]] gives normalized coordinates directly. The outer frame, as large as
the pane, only centers a small image in it. A tap focuses the variant without touching the line.

<<ImageCanvas: drawing the image at true scale>>=
var body: some View {
    let scale = session.useDevicePixels ? displayScale : 1
    let size = CGSize(width: CGFloat(variant.buffer.width) / scale, height: CGFloat(variant.buffer.height) / scale)
    GeometryReader { geo in
        ScrollView([.horizontal, .vertical]) {
            ZStack {
                Image(decorative: variant.cgImage, scale: scale)
                    .interpolation(.none)
                LineOverlay(line: session.line)
                    .allowsHitTesting(false)
            }
            .frame(width: size.width, height: size.height)
            .contentShape(Rectangle())
            .gesture(lineGesture(size: size))
            .simultaneousGesture(TapGesture().onEnded { session.focusedVariantID = variant.id })
            .frame(minWidth: geo.size.width, minHeight: geo.size.height)
        }
    }
    .background(Color(nsColor: .underPageBackgroundColor))
}
@ The drag decides its mode on its first change and remembers it until it ends. A minimum distance of
two points keeps a plain click from being taken as a zero-length new line. Writing
[[session.line]] is all it takes to move the line in every pane of every zone.

<<ImageCanvas: the line gesture>>=
private func lineGesture(size: CGSize) -> some Gesture {
    DragGesture(minimumDistance: 2)
        .onChanged { g in
            if drag == nil {
                drag = DragState(mode: hitTest(g.startLocation, size: size), initial: session.line)
                session.focusedVariantID = variant.id
            }
            guard let drag else { return }
            session.line = updatedLine(drag, gesture: g, size: size)
        }
        .onEnded { _ in drag = nil }
}
@ Hit testing works in points, so the targets are the same size whatever the image's scale. The
endpoints come first and are a little more generous (9 points) than the segment (6 points), so an
endpoint can always be grabbed even where it touches the segment. A drag that starts anywhere else
draws a new line.

<<ImageCanvas: hit testing the line>>=
private func hitTest(_ p: CGPoint, size: CGSize) -> DragState.Mode {
    let a = point(session.line.start, size)
    let b = point(session.line.end, size)
    if distance(p, a) <= 9 { return .start }
    if distance(p, b) <= 9 { return .end }
    if distanceToSegment(p, a, b) <= 6 { return .whole }
    return .draw
}
@ Moving an endpoint or drawing a new line places a point where the pointer is. That point has been
clamped to the image, so a line can reach the image's edge but never pass it.

<<ImageCanvas: updating the line during a drag>>=
private func updatedLine(_ drag: DragState, gesture g: DragGesture.Value, size: CGSize) -> LineSegment {
    let shift = NSEvent.modifierFlags.contains(.shift)
    let current = normalized(g.location, size)
    var line = drag.initial
    switch drag.mode {
    case .start:
        line.start = snapped(current, anchor: line.end, size: size, enabled: shift)
    case .end:
        line.end = snapped(current, anchor: line.start, size: size, enabled: shift)
    case .draw:
        line.start = normalized(g.startLocation, size)
        line.end = snapped(current, anchor: line.start, size: size, enabled: shift)
    case .whole:
        <<ImageCanvas: moving the whole segment without leaving the image>>
    }
    return line
}
@ Moving the whole segment is different. Clamping each endpoint separately would squash the segment
against the edge and change its length and angle. Instead the \emph{offset} is clamped, so that both
endpoints stay inside. If the initial endpoints have $x$ coordinates $x_s$ and $x_e$, the
horizontal offset $\Delta x$ must satisfy
\[
  -\min(x_s, x_e) \;\le\; \Delta x \;\le\; 1 - \max(x_s, x_e),
\]
and the same holds vertically. Within that range the segment keeps its shape exactly; at the limit
it slides along the edge.

<<ImageCanvas: moving the whole segment without leaving the image>>=
// Move both endpoints, limiting the offset so neither leaves the image.
var dx = g.translation.width / size.width
var dy = g.translation.height / size.height
let i = drag.initial
dx = min(max(dx, -min(i.start.x, i.end.x)), 1 - max(i.start.x, i.end.x))
dy = min(max(dy, -min(i.start.y, i.end.y)), 1 - max(i.start.y, i.end.y))
line.start = NormalizedPoint(x: i.start.x + dx, y: i.start.y + dy)
line.end = NormalizedPoint(x: i.end.x + dx, y: i.end.y + dy)
@ Holding Shift makes the segment exactly horizontal or vertical, which is what a profile along a
row or a column needs. Which of the two is decided by the larger displacement \emph{in pixels}, not
in normalized units. In a wide image a normalized step of $0.1$ across is much longer than $0.1$
down, and comparing normalized values would snap the wrong way.

<<ImageCanvas: Shift snapping>>=
/// With Shift held, constrains the segment to horizontal or vertical (in pixel space).
private func snapped(_ p: NormalizedPoint, anchor: NormalizedPoint, size: CGSize, enabled: Bool) -> NormalizedPoint {
    guard enabled else { return p }
    let dx = abs(p.x - anchor.x) * size.width
    let dy = abs(p.y - anchor.y) * size.height
    return dx >= dy ? NormalizedPoint(x: p.x, y: anchor.y) : NormalizedPoint(x: anchor.x, y: p.y)
}
@ The remaining helpers convert between points and normalized coordinates, and measure distances.
The distance to a segment projects the point onto the segment's line, with the parameter $t$
clamped to $[0,1]$ so the nearest point stays between the endpoints. A degenerate segment is
treated as a point.

<<ImageCanvas: coordinate conversions>>=
private func normalized(_ p: CGPoint, _ size: CGSize) -> NormalizedPoint {
    NormalizedPoint(x: p.x / size.width, y: p.y / size.height).clamped()
}

private func point(_ n: NormalizedPoint, _ size: CGSize) -> CGPoint {
    CGPoint(x: n.x * size.width, y: n.y * size.height)
}

private func distance(_ a: CGPoint, _ b: CGPoint) -> CGFloat {
    hypot(a.x - b.x, a.y - b.y)
}

private func distanceToSegment(_ p: CGPoint, _ a: CGPoint, _ b: CGPoint) -> CGFloat {
    let ab = CGPoint(x: b.x - a.x, y: b.y - a.y)
    let len2 = ab.x * ab.x + ab.y * ab.y
    guard len2 > 0 else { return distance(p, a) }
    let t = min(max(((p.x - a.x) * ab.x + (p.y - a.y) * ab.y) / len2, 0), 1)
    return distance(p, CGPoint(x: a.x + ab.x * t, y: a.y + ab.y * t))
}
@ The overlay is a [[Canvas]] the size of the image, so normalized coordinates become points by a
single multiplication. The line has to be visible on any image, light or dark. So it is drawn
twice: a wide dark stroke with a thin yellow stroke on top. The two handles differ on purpose. The
filled one is the start, position $0$ on the profile's horizontal axis, and the hollow one is the
end. Without that, a profile read from right to left would be easy to misread.

<<ImageCanvas.swift: the line overlay>>=
/// Draws the selection line: a filled handle at the start (position 0) and a hollow one at the end.
private struct LineOverlay: View {
    let line: LineSegment

    var body: some View {
        Canvas { ctx, size in
            let a = CGPoint(x: line.start.x * size.width, y: line.start.y * size.height)
            let b = CGPoint(x: line.end.x * size.width, y: line.end.y * size.height)
            var path = Path()
            path.move(to: a)
            path.addLine(to: b)
            ctx.stroke(path, with: .color(.black.opacity(0.7)), lineWidth: 3)
            ctx.stroke(path, with: .color(.yellow), lineWidth: 1.25)

            let r: CGFloat = 5
            let startDot = Path(ellipseIn: CGRect(x: a.x - r, y: a.y - r, width: 2 * r, height: 2 * r))
            ctx.fill(startDot, with: .color(.yellow))
            ctx.stroke(startDot, with: .color(.black.opacity(0.7)), lineWidth: 1)
            let endDot = Path(ellipseIn: CGRect(x: b.x - r, y: b.y - r, width: 2 * r, height: 2 * r))
            ctx.fill(endDot, with: .color(.black.opacity(0.35)))
            ctx.stroke(endDot, with: .color(.yellow), lineWidth: 1.5)
        }
    }
}
@

\section{Charts}

Both charts use Swift Charts' vectorized [[LinePlot]], which draws thousands of points quickly
enough to follow a drag. Each chart computes its data in [[body]], from the session. Because the
session is observable, any change to the line, the channel or the spectrum settings redraws the
chart.

<<LineScope/Views/Charts.swift>>=
import Charts
import LineScopeCore
import SwiftUI

<<Charts.swift: the profile chart>>

<<Charts.swift: the spectrum chart>>
@ The profile's horizontal axis is the position along the line, from $0$ at the start handle to $1$
at the end. The number of samples depends on the variant: an image at half size gives half as many
samples along the same line. So the axis label reports the count. Using normalized position lets
profiles of differently sized copies line up side by side.

The vertical axis is \emph{fixed} to the channel's natural range: $[0,1]$ for most channels, and
$[0,360]$ degrees for hue. If each chart chose its own range, a faint ripple and a full-contrast
square wave would look alike. With a shared, fixed domain, the charts of different variants can be
compared by eye.

<<Charts.swift: the profile chart>>=
/// Zone 2: the selected channel sampled along the line in this variant's own pixel grid.
struct ProfileChart: View {
    let variant: ImageVariant
    let session: Session

    var body: some View {
        let profile = session.profile(for: variant)
        let channel = session.channelName
        Chart {
            LinePlot(profile.points, x: .value("Position", \.x), y: .value(channel, \.y))
                .foregroundStyle(channelColor)
                .lineStyle(StrokeStyle(lineWidth: 1.2))
        }
        .chartXScale(domain: 0...1)
        .chartYScale(domain: session.colorModel.range(ofChannel: session.channelIndex))
        .chartXAxisLabel("Position along line (\(profile.samples.colors.count) samples)")
        .chartYAxisLabel(channel)
        .padding(10)
    }

    <<ProfileChart: a color for each channel>>
}
@ The curve takes the color of its channel, so a glance tells which channel is plotted. Channels
without a natural hue (alpha, lightness, black) use the primary text color.

<<ProfileChart: a color for each channel>>=
private var channelColor: Color {
    switch (session.colorModel, session.channelIndex) {
    case (.rgba, 0): .red
    case (.rgba, 1): .green
    case (.rgba, 2): .blue
    case (.cmyk, 0): .cyan
    case (.cmyk, 1): .pink
    case (.cmyk, 2): .yellow
    case (.hsl, 0): .purple
    case (.hsl, 1): .orange
    default: .primary
    }
}
@ The spectrum chart transforms the same profile. Its horizontal axis ends at the Nyquist
frequency, the highest frequency the samples can represent, and a dashed rule marks it. In
cycles per pixel, Nyquist is about $\frac12$ for every variant, since the samples are about one
pixel apart. In cycles per line it is half the number of samples, so it moves: a copy at half size
has its Nyquist limit at half the frequency. Energy that the original has above that limit either
disappears (a good low-pass filter) or folds back below it (aliasing). The rule shows at once which
side of the limit a feature is on.

<<Charts.swift: the spectrum chart>>=
/// Zone 3: amplitude spectrum of the zone-2 profile.
struct SpectrumChart: View {
    let variant: ImageVariant
    let session: Session

    var body: some View {
        let profile = session.profile(for: variant)
        let spectrum = session.spectrum(for: profile)
        let points = session.spectrumPoints(spectrum)
        let nyquist = nyquist(spectrum)
        Chart {
            LinePlot(points, x: .value("Frequency", \.frequency), y: .value("Amplitude", \.magnitude))
                .foregroundStyle(Color.accentColor)
                .lineStyle(StrokeStyle(lineWidth: 1.2))
            if let nyquist {
                RuleMark(x: .value("Nyquist", nyquist))
                    .foregroundStyle(.secondary)
                    .lineStyle(StrokeStyle(lineWidth: 1, dash: [4, 3]))
                    .annotation(position: .leading, alignment: .top) {
                        Text("Nyquist").font(.caption2).foregroundStyle(.secondary)
                    }
            }
        }
        .chartXScale(domain: 0...max(nyquist ?? 0.5, 1e-9))
        .chartYScale(domain: yDomain(points))
        .chartXAxisLabel("Frequency (\(session.frequencyAxis.displayName)), FFT size \(spectrum.fftSize)")
        .chartYAxisLabel(session.logMagnitude ? "Amplitude (dB)" : "Amplitude")
        .padding(10)
    }

    <<SpectrumChart: the Nyquist frequency on the current axis>>

    <<SpectrumChart: the amplitude range>>
}
@ The last bin of the one-sided spectrum is the Nyquist bin, so its frequency, in whichever unit the
axis uses, is the limit. An empty spectrum (a line too short to sample) has no limit.

<<SpectrumChart: the Nyquist frequency on the current axis>>=
private func nyquist(_ s: SpectrumResult) -> Double? {
    guard s.fftSize > 0 else { return nil }
    switch session.frequencyAxis {
    case .cyclesPerPixel: return s.cyclesPerPixel.last
    case .cyclesPerLine: return s.cyclesPerLine.last
    }
}
@ In decibels, the vertical range is fixed at the bottom at $-100$\,dB, the floor to which the
session clips tiny amplitudes. The top is the next multiple of 10\,dB above the peak (at least 0),
so charts of different variants mostly share one scale. On the linear scale the peak sets the
range, with 5\% of headroom.

<<SpectrumChart: the amplitude range>>=
private func yDomain(_ points: [SpectrumPoint]) -> ClosedRange<Double> {
    let top = points.map(\.magnitude).max() ?? 1
    if session.logMagnitude {
        return -100...max(0, (top / 10).rounded(.up) * 10)
    }
    return 0...max(top * 1.05, 1e-6)
}
@

\section{The parameter sheet}\label{sec:parameter-sheet}

Operations that take parameters (the blurs, sharpening, noise reduction and every resampling
method) ask for them in a sheet before running. The menu only sets the session's
[[pendingRequest]]; the window presents this sheet for it; and Apply hands a finished
[[ImageOperation]] to the session, which runs it on the focused variant in the background.

<<LineScope/Views/ParameterSheet.swift>>=
import LineScopeCore
import SwiftUI

/// Collects parameters for a filter or resample, then runs it on the focused variant.
struct ParameterSheet: View {
    let request: OperationRequest
    let session: Session
    @Environment(\.dismiss) private var dismiss

    <<ParameterSheet: parameters and their defaults>>

    <<ParameterSheet: layout>>

    <<ParameterSheet: fields for each request>>

    <<ParameterSheet: a numeric field>>

    <<ParameterSheet: validating a resample>>

    <<ParameterSheet: building the operation>>
}
@ One sheet serves every request, so it simply holds all the parameters any of them might need,
with defaults that give a visible but moderate effect. A scale of $0.5$ is the most instructive
default for resampling: halving an image is where aliasing first shows.

<<ParameterSheet: parameters and their defaults>>=
@State private var radius = 2.0
@State private var intensity = 0.5
@State private var noiseLevel = 0.02
@State private var sharpness = 0.4
@State private var scale = 0.5

private static let maxOutputSide = 16_384
@ The sheet names the variant it will act on. Operations chain, so the source is often not the
original, and the user should be able to see which variant it is. Return applies and Escape cancels.

<<ParameterSheet: layout>>=
var body: some View {
    let source = session.focusedVariant
    VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 14) {
        Text(request.title).font(.headline)
        Text("Applies to: \(source.name) (\(source.buffer.width)×\(source.buffer.height))")
            .font(.caption)
            .foregroundStyle(.secondary)

        Form { fields(source: source) }

        HStack {
            Spacer()
            Button("Cancel", role: .cancel) { dismiss() }
                .keyboardShortcut(.cancelAction)
            Button("Apply") {
                session.perform(operation)
                dismiss()
            }
            .keyboardShortcut(.defaultAction)
            .disabled(!isValid(source: source))
        }
    }
    .padding(20)
    .frame(width: 380)
}
@ Each request shows only its own fields. For resampling, the sheet shows the resulting size as the
scale is typed, computed with the same rounding the resampler will use
([[Resampler.outputSize]]). What the user sees is exactly what the operation will produce.

<<ParameterSheet: fields for each request>>=
@ViewBuilder
private func fields(source: ImageVariant) -> some View {
    switch request {
    case .gaussianBlur, .boxBlur:
        number("Radius (px)", $radius, range: 0...100)
    case .unsharpMask:
        number("Radius (px)", $radius, range: 0...100)
        number("Intensity", $intensity, range: 0...10)
    case .noiseReduction:
        number("Noise level", $noiseLevel, range: 0...0.1)
        number("Sharpness", $sharpness, range: 0...2)
    case .resample:
        LabeledContent("Scale factor") {
            HStack {
                TextField("Scale", value: $scale, format: .number.precision(.fractionLength(0...4)))
                    .frame(width: 80)
                Menu("Presets") {
                    ForEach([0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.5, 2, 4], id: \.self) { s in
                        Button("×\(s.formatted())") { scale = s }
                    }
                }
                .fixedSize()
            }
        }
        LabeledContent("Result") {
            Text("\(Resampler.outputSize(for: source.buffer.width, scale: scale)) × \(Resampler.outputSize(for: source.buffer.height, scale: scale)) px")
                .monospacedDigit()
        }
    }
}
@ A numeric parameter is a slider for exploring together with a text field for exact values.
Both are bound to the same state.

<<ParameterSheet: a numeric field>>=
private func number(_ label: String, _ value: Binding<Double>, range: ClosedRange<Double>) -> some View {
    LabeledContent(label) {
        HStack {
            Slider(value: value, in: range)
            TextField(label, value: value, format: .number.precision(.fractionLength(0...3)))
                .frame(width: 60)
                .labelsHidden()
        }
    }
}
@ Only resampling can be asked for something unreasonable: a zero or negative scale, or an output so
large it would exhaust memory. With four [[Float]] components per pixel, a $16384\times16384$
image already takes 4\,GiB, so that is the limit.

<<ParameterSheet: validating a resample>>=
private func isValid(source: ImageVariant) -> Bool {
    guard case .resample = request else { return true }
    let w = Resampler.outputSize(for: source.buffer.width, scale: scale)
    let h = Resampler.outputSize(for: source.buffer.height, scale: scale)
    return scale > 0 && max(w, h) <= Self.maxOutputSide
}
@ Finally the request and the collected values become an operation, a plain value that can run
anywhere, record itself in a variant's lineage, and name the new tab.

<<ParameterSheet: building the operation>>=
private var operation: ImageOperation {
    switch request {
    case .gaussianBlur: .filter(.gaussianBlur(radius: radius))
    case .boxBlur: .filter(.boxBlur(radius: radius))
    case .unsharpMask: .filter(.unsharpMask(radius: radius, intensity: intensity))
    case .noiseReduction: .filter(.noiseReduction(level: noiseLevel, sharpness: sharpness))
    case .resample(let method): .resample(method, scale: scale)
    }
}
@

\section{The inspector}

The inspector is a separate window that describes the focused variant of the most recently active
document window. As explained with [[MainView]], it can't find that window through focused values.
The inspector \emph{is} the key window while it is being read or scrolled, and then the focused
value would be empty. It observes [[ActiveSession.shared]] instead, which document windows update
when they become key.

<<LineScope/Views/InspectorView.swift>>=
import LineScopeCore
import SwiftUI

/// Separate window describing the focused variant of the most recently active document window.
struct InspectorView: View {
    static let windowID = "inspector"

    private var active = ActiveSession.shared

    var body: some View {
        Group {
            if let session = active.session {
                InspectorContent(session: session)
            } else {
                ContentUnavailableView("No Image", systemImage: "photo", description: Text("Open an image to inspect it."))
            }
        }
        .frame(minWidth: 300, idealWidth: 340, minHeight: 320, idealHeight: 760)
    }
}

<<InspectorView.swift: the inspector's content>>
@ The content is a grouped form of read-only rows. It computes the same profile and spectrum as the
charts, so its numbers always agree with what the zones show.

<<InspectorView.swift: the inspector's content>>=
private struct InspectorContent: View {
    let session: Session

    var body: some View {
        let variant = session.focusedVariant
        let profile = session.profile(for: variant)
        let spectrum = session.spectrum(for: profile)
        let info = session.sourceInfo
        let original = session.original.buffer

        Form {
            <<InspectorContent: the file section>>

            <<InspectorContent: the focused-image section>>

            <<InspectorContent: the operation-chain section>>

            <<InspectorContent: the line section>>

            <<InspectorContent: the channel-statistics section>>
        }
        .formStyle(.grouped)
    }

    <<InspectorContent: row and number helpers>>
}
@ The file section describes the image as it was stored, before it was converted to the float
buffer all processing uses. A 16-bit or CMYK file shows that here, even though every variant is
processed in the same format.

<<InspectorContent: the file section>>=
Section("File") {
    row("Name", session.fileName)
    row("Type", info.fileType)
    row("Stored size", "\(info.pixelWidth) × \(info.pixelHeight) px")
    row("Bit depth", "\(info.bitsPerComponent) bpc, \(info.bitsPerPixel) bpp")
    row("Color space", info.colorSpaceName)
    row("Alpha", info.hasAlpha ? "Yes" : "No")
    if let dpi = info.dpi { row("Resolution", "\(num(dpi, 0)) dpi") }
}
@ For the focused variant, the scale is given separately for each axis. Rounding to whole pixels
makes a nominal $\times0.5$ of an odd-sized image slightly different horizontally and vertically.

<<InspectorContent: the focused-image section>>=
Section("Focused Image") {
    row("Tab", variant.name)
    row("Size", "\(variant.buffer.width) × \(variant.buffer.height) px")
    row(
        "Scale vs. original",
        "×\(num(Double(variant.buffer.width) / Double(original.width), 4)) · ×\(num(Double(variant.buffer.height) / Double(original.height), 4))"
    )
    row("Working format", "RGBA Float32, sRGB-encoded")
}
@ The operation chain lists, in order, every step from the original to this variant. This is the
full history, even though each step was applied to whatever variant was focused at the time.

<<InspectorContent: the operation-chain section>>=
Section("Operation Chain") {
    Text("Original").foregroundStyle(.secondary)
    ForEach(Array(variant.lineage.enumerated()), id: \.offset) { i, op in
        Text("\(i + 1). \(op.displayName)")
    }
}
@ The line is shared in normalized coordinates, but here it is shown in \emph{this} variant's
pixels. Its length, sample count and spacing follow, so you can see directly that a resampled
copy is sampled more coarsely along the same line.

<<InspectorContent: the line section>>=
Section("Line") {
    let w = Double(variant.buffer.width), h = Double(variant.buffer.height)
    let l = session.line
    row("Start", "(\(num(l.start.x * w, 1)), \(num(l.start.y * h, 1))) px")
    row("End", "(\(num(l.end.x * w, 1)), \(num(l.end.y * h, 1))) px")
    row("Length", "\(num(profile.samples.pixelLength, 1)) px")
    row("Samples", "\(profile.values.count), spacing \(num(profile.samples.spacing, 3)) px")
}
@ Last come summary statistics of the profile and the strongest frequency in its spectrum, given in
both units of the frequency axis. With the mean removed, that peak is the dominant periodicity
along the line: for a grating, its frequency.

<<InspectorContent: the channel-statistics section>>=
Section("\(session.colorModel.displayName) · \(session.channelName)") {
    let v = profile.values
    row("Min", num(Double(v.min() ?? 0), 4))
    row("Max", num(Double(v.max() ?? 0), 4))
    row("Mean", num(Double(v.reduce(0, +)) / Double(max(v.count, 1)), 4))
    if let k = spectrum.peakIndex {
        row("Peak frequency", "\(num(spectrum.cyclesPerPixel[k], 4)) c/px · \(num(spectrum.cyclesPerLine[k], 2)) c/line")
        row("Peak amplitude", num(spectrum.amplitudes[k], 4))
    }
}
@ Values are selectable so they can be copied, and numbers are formatted with a fixed number of
decimals in the user's locale.

<<InspectorContent: row and number helpers>>=
private func row(_ label: String, _ value: String) -> some View {
    LabeledContent(label) {
        Text(value).textSelection(.enabled).multilineTextAlignment(.trailing)
    }
}

private func num(_ v: Double, _ digits: Int) -> String {
    v.formatted(.number.precision(.fractionLength(digits)))
}
@

\section{Test-pattern windows}

A generated test pattern (Chapter~\ref{ch:patterns}) is shown in exactly the same analysis window
as an image file. It is only a different way of producing the [[ImageDocument]]. Two views are
needed: the window for a pattern, and a generator window where a pattern is designed before it is
created.

<<LineScope/Views/TestPatternViews.swift>>=
import LineScopeCore
import SwiftUI

<<TestPatternViews.swift: the pattern analysis window>>

<<TestPatternViews.swift: the generator window>>
@ A pattern window receives only the pattern's [[TestPatternSpec]], a small value that SwiftUI can
store and restore with the window. The image must be generated from it, and that must happen
\emph{once}. Doing it in [[init]] or [[body]] would regenerate a possibly large image on every
redraw, and a fresh document would also mean a fresh session, losing all derived variants. So the
document lives in [[@State]] and is filled by a task keyed on the spec, which runs once when the
window appears. The generation itself runs detached, off the main thread, while a progress
indicator holds the window's place.

<<TestPatternViews.swift: the pattern analysis window>>=
/// Analysis window for a generated pattern. Generation runs once per window, not on every view update.
struct PatternWindow: View {
    let spec: TestPatternSpec
    @State private var document: ImageDocument?

    var body: some View {
        Group {
            if let document {
                MainView(document: document, fileName: spec.title)
            } else {
                ProgressView("Generating \(spec.kind.displayName)…")
                    .frame(minWidth: 720, minHeight: 560)
            }
        }
        .navigationTitle(spec.title)
        .task(id: spec) {
            let spec = spec
            document = await Task.detached(priority: .userInitiated) { ImageDocument(pattern: spec) }.value
        }
    }
}
@ The generator window edits a spec: the kind of pattern, its size, and its one parameter. It shows
a live preview, and Create opens a pattern window for it.

<<TestPatternViews.swift: the generator window>>=
/// Window for choosing a pattern, its size and parameter before creating it.
struct TestPatternGeneratorView: View {
    static let windowID = "pattern-generator"

    @Environment(\.openWindow) private var openWindow
    @State private var spec = TestPatternSpec(kind: .zonePlate)
    @State private var preview: CGImage?

    private static let sizes = [128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048]
    private static let maxSide = 8192
    private static let maxPreviewPixels = 2048 * 2048

    var body: some View {
        HStack(alignment: .top, spacing: 20) {
            Form {
                <<Generator window: pattern picker and description>>

                <<Generator window: size fields>>

                <<Generator window: parameter field>>

                <<Generator window: the create button>>
            }
            .formStyle(.grouped)
            .frame(width: 380)

            previewView
        }
        .padding(16)
        .task(id: spec) {
            <<Generator window: rendering the preview>>
        }
    }

    <<Generator window: the preview box>>

    <<Generator window: changing the pattern kind>>

    <<Generator window: validation and clamping>>
}
@ Each kind of pattern carries a sentence on what it is good for. Showing that under the picker
turns the list into a small catalogue of test targets.

<<Generator window: pattern picker and description>>=
Picker("Pattern", selection: kindBinding) {
    ForEach(TestPatternKind.allCases) { Text($0.displayName).tag($0) }
}
Text(spec.kind.purpose)
    .font(.caption)
    .foregroundStyle(.secondary)
    .fixedSize(horizontal: false, vertical: true)
@ The size is typed or picked from square presets. Grouping separators are turned off, so a width
reads $2048$ and not $2{,}048$.

<<Generator window: size fields>>=
LabeledContent("Size") {
    HStack {
        TextField("Width", value: $spec.width, format: .number.grouping(.never))
            .frame(width: 64)
        Text("×")
        TextField("Height", value: $spec.height, format: .number.grouping(.never))
            .frame(width: 64)
        Menu("Presets") {
            ForEach(Self.sizes, id: \.self) { s in
                Button("\(s) × \(s)") { spec.width = s; spec.height = s }
            }
        }
        .fixedSize()
    }
    .labelsHidden()
}
@ The parameter's name and range come from the pattern kind itself. Kinds without a parameter show
nothing. The noise seed gets no slider: neighboring seeds are unrelated, so dragging through them
means nothing.

<<Generator window: parameter field>>=
if let p = spec.kind.parameter {
    LabeledContent(p.name) {
        HStack {
            if spec.kind != .whiteNoise {
                Slider(value: $spec.parameter, in: p.range)
            }
            TextField(p.name, value: $spec.parameter, format: .number.precision(.fractionLength(0...4)))
                .frame(width: 70)
                .labelsHidden()
        }
    }
}
@ Create opens a new pattern window \emph{by value}. SwiftUI finds the window group for
[[TestPatternSpec]], and creating the same spec twice brings the existing window forward instead
of opening a duplicate. A typed parameter can lie outside the slider's range, so it is clamped
first.

<<Generator window: the create button>>=
HStack {
    Spacer()
    Button("Create") { openWindow(value: clamped(spec)) }
        .keyboardShortcut(.defaultAction)
        .disabled(!isValid)
}
@ The preview must show what the pattern will really look like. Generating a small version of the
pattern would be wrong: a zone plate made at $256\times256$ is a different image from the center of
one made at $1024\times1024$, with different frequencies and different aliasing. So the preview
generates the full pattern and crops its central $256\times256$ pixels, which are shown at 1:1.
Huge patterns are not previewed at all.

The task is keyed on the spec, so SwiftUI cancels it whenever the spec changes. The short sleep
debounces typing and slider drags: only a spec that stays unchanged for 80\,ms is rendered.

<<Generator window: rendering the preview>>=
guard isValid, spec.width * spec.height <= Self.maxPreviewPixels else { preview = nil; return }
// Generate the real pattern and show its central pixels at 1:1, so aliasing looks as it will
// in the analysis window.
let s = clamped(spec)
try? await Task.sleep(for: .milliseconds(80))
guard !Task.isCancelled else { return }
preview = await Task.detached {
    guard let full = TestPatternGenerator.make(s).makeCGImage() else { return nil }
    let w = min(s.width, 256), h = min(s.height, 256)
    let rect = CGRect(x: (s.width - w) / 2, y: (s.height - h) / 2, width: w, height: h)
    return full.cropping(to: rect)
}.value
@ The preview box always has the same size, so the window doesn't jump as specs change. As in the
image canvas, interpolation is off: a preview pixel is an image pixel.

<<Generator window: the preview box>>=
private var previewView: some View {
    VStack(spacing: 6) {
        Group {
            if let preview {
                Image(decorative: preview, scale: 1).interpolation(.none)
            } else {
                Color.clear
            }
        }
        .frame(width: 256, height: 256)
        .background(Color(nsColor: .underPageBackgroundColor))
        .clipShape(RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 4))
        Text(spec.width * spec.height <= Self.maxPreviewPixels
             ? "Preview: center 256 × 256 px at 1:1" : "Too large to preview")
            .font(.caption)
            .foregroundStyle(.secondary)
    }
}
@ Parameters mean different things for different patterns: a frequency in cycles per pixel for a
chirp, a period in pixels for bars, a number of spokes for the star. Carrying the value over when
the kind changes would produce nonsense, such as a checkerboard with cells $0.5$ pixels wide. So
the custom binding rebuilds the spec, keeping the size and taking the new kind's default parameter.

<<Generator window: changing the pattern kind>>=
/// Resets the parameter to the new pattern's default when the kind changes.
private var kindBinding: Binding<TestPatternKind> {
    Binding(
        get: { spec.kind },
        set: { spec = TestPatternSpec(kind: $0, width: spec.width, height: spec.height) }
    )
}
@ Sizes are limited to 8192 pixels per side, and the parameter is limited to its kind's range.

<<Generator window: validation and clamping>>=
private var isValid: Bool {
    (1...Self.maxSide).contains(spec.width) && (1...Self.maxSide).contains(spec.height)
}

private func clamped(_ s: TestPatternSpec) -> TestPatternSpec {
    var s = s
    if let p = s.kind.parameter {
        s.parameter = min(max(s.parameter, p.range.lowerBound), p.range.upperBound)
    }
    return s
}
@
